1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,803
Hi, I'm Junsu.

2
00:00:00,803 --> 00:00:03,785
This is Dock Risk, built for the RoadStar Hackathon.

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:06,195
It tells a dispatcher which truck to move,

4
00:00:06,195 --> 00:00:08,259
while there's still time to move it.

5
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,820
A truck waits at a dock.

6
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:12,182
Two clocks are running.

7
00:00:12,182 --> 00:00:12,951
One is money.

8
00:00:12,951 --> 00:00:15,378
Past two hours, detention starts billing.

9
00:00:15,378 --> 00:00:16,621
The other is the law.

10
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:18,456
Waiting counts as on-duty time,

11
00:00:18,456 --> 00:00:21,120
so his legal hours drain while he sits still.

12
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,372
Here's the part nobody watches.

13
00:00:24,372 --> 00:00:26,511
The second clock runs out first.

14
00:00:26,511 --> 00:00:29,519
In this scenario the next load dies at 12:53.

15
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:31,792
Free time doesn't end until 13:30.

16
00:00:31,792 --> 00:00:33,196
Thirty-seven minutes.

17
00:00:33,196 --> 00:00:36,739
Every detention tool on the market wakes up at 13:30.

18
00:00:37,799 --> 00:00:38,902
So here's the desk.

19
00:00:38,902 --> 00:00:39,657
Eight trucks,

20
00:00:39,657 --> 00:00:42,500
ranked by which one is about to become a problem.

21
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,879
The first two columns are the two clocks.

22
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:46,929
Open Driver84.

23
00:00:46,929 --> 00:00:48,721
He's been waiting two hours.

24
00:00:48,721 --> 00:00:51,281
Detention starts in forty-three minutes.

25
00:00:51,281 --> 00:00:54,353
He has fifty-four minutes to reach a legal stop.

26
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:57,576
And here is the part that matters.

27
00:00:57,576 --> 00:00:59,979
It doesn't tell you the load is at risk.

28
00:00:59,979 --> 00:01:01,421
It tells you what to do.

29
00:01:01,421 --> 00:01:03,704
Reassign four-one-one-nine-oh-two now.

30
00:01:03,704 --> 00:01:06,108
The wait has already made it impossible.

31
00:01:06,108 --> 00:01:09,532
Release him this second and he is still forty-six minutes

32
00:01:09,532 --> 00:01:10,132
short.

33
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:12,974
The driver confirms his check-in.

34
00:01:12,974 --> 00:01:13,574
One tap.

35
00:01:13,444 --> 00:01:14,679
That tap is evidence.

36
00:01:14,679 --> 00:01:16,914
It sets when the billing clock starts,

37
00:01:16,914 --> 00:01:18,973
and it's kept with who reported it.

38
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:21,683
So you find a relief driver.

39
00:01:21,683 --> 00:01:24,395
Candidates come back with reasons, not scores.

40
00:01:24,395 --> 00:01:26,576
Distance, arrival time, trailer type,

41
00:01:26,576 --> 00:01:29,111
and whether his hours cover the whole trip.

42
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:32,353
Drivers who can't make it are blocked, and it says why.

43
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:34,695
One offer goes out.

44
00:01:34,695 --> 00:01:36,652
The driver accepts on his phone.

45
00:01:36,652 --> 00:01:37,753
The load is saved.

46
00:01:37,753 --> 00:01:40,933
The original stop keeps every piece of its evidence.

47
00:01:42,099 --> 00:01:45,740
Now Driver84 is released, and the charge writes itself.

48
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,395
Fifteen billable minutes.

49
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:48,851
Eighteen seventy-five.

50
00:01:48,851 --> 00:01:50,439
Billed to the consignee.

51
00:01:51,599 --> 00:01:53,476
This is what makes it collectable.

52
00:01:53,476 --> 00:01:56,787
The amount, then the arithmetic with every rule spelled out,

53
00:01:56,787 --> 00:01:59,381
then the ledger each event and who reported it.

54
00:01:59,381 --> 00:02:01,644
When the evidence is thin, we don't bill.

55
00:02:01,644 --> 00:02:04,679
It's drafted, flagged, and the packet says exactly why.

56
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:06,715
One more thing,

57
00:02:06,715 --> 00:02:10,124
and it's the one a dispatcher can act on tomorrow morning.

58
00:02:10,124 --> 00:02:12,652
The hour you send a truck changes the odds.

59
00:02:12,652 --> 00:02:13,710
In their own data,

60
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:16,885
a truck arriving at seven in the morning runs over the

61
00:02:16,885 --> 00:02:20,353
two-hour line three times as often as one arriving at seven

62
00:02:20,353 --> 00:02:20,953
at night.

63
00:02:20,882 --> 00:02:21,529
Same docks.

64
00:02:21,529 --> 00:02:22,411
Same customers.

65
00:02:22,411 --> 00:02:25,644
So the desk stops saying "this dock is slow" and starts

66
00:02:25,644 --> 00:02:28,113
saying "don't book this dock before noon."

67
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:31,635
We replayed the organizers' own export.

68
00:02:31,635 --> 00:02:34,036
Fifty-six days of real dock timestamps.

69
00:02:34,036 --> 00:02:37,362
About thirty-one thousand dollars a month of exposure,

70
00:02:37,362 --> 00:02:40,995
and the warning caught a hundred and fifteen of the hundred

71
00:02:40,995 --> 00:02:43,212
and twenty-six stops that went over.

72
00:02:43,212 --> 00:02:46,722
We trained on the first four weeks and scored on the last

73
00:02:46,722 --> 00:02:47,322
four.

74
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:49,309
We also named what we couldn't build.

75
00:02:49,309 --> 00:02:52,696
Axle weight isn't possible — that sheet has one column.

76
00:02:52,696 --> 00:02:54,790
And we say exposure, not unbilled,

77
00:02:54,790 --> 00:02:57,253
because the file has no billing records.

78
00:02:58,299 --> 00:03:00,415
Detention timers are table stakes.

79
00:03:00,415 --> 00:03:02,966
Telling a dispatcher which truck to move,

80
00:03:02,966 --> 00:03:04,459
early enough to move it.

81
00:03:04,459 --> 00:03:05,268
That is Dock.

82
00:03:05,268 --> 00:03:05,868
Risk.

83
00:03:05,579 --> 00:03:07,819
Watch the clock that runs out first.

